# Line endings, and some of these are load-bearing.
#
# This machine has `core.autocrlf=true`, so without a rule git checks text out
# with CRLF. For source that is harmless: rustc reads CRLF and LF alike, and the
# exporter's parity legs have always run against a CRLF working tree. For the
# files below it is not.
#
# **No `* text=auto eol=lf` here, although the analyser had one.** It came over
# with the analyser in the TelegramTools merge, and applying it repo-wide would
# have changed the line endings of every exporter file on the next checkout --
# including the HTML assets `tgx-html` copies into an export -- in a step whose
# whole point was to change no output byte. The analyser's specific rules carry
# over instead, and they are the ones that mattered.

# **The analyser's goldens and recorded fixtures are compared byte for byte**,
# against strings the program builds with `\n`. Checked out with CRLF they differ
# from the render on every line, and the failure reads as "the report drifted"
# on a fresh clone that has changed nothing. `-text` disables conversion in both
# directions, so what is committed is what lands on disk.
crates/tga-report/tests/golden.html           -text
crates/tga-report/tests/synthetic/stats.json  -text
crates/tga-report/tests/synthetic/result.json -text
crates/tga-report/tests/synthetic/events.json -text
crates/tga-report/tests/fixture.stats.json    -text

# **The report's stylesheet and script are copied into every report verbatim**,
# so they are the same case one step earlier: checked out with CRLF, every report
# differs from the golden on 500 lines and nothing in the source has changed.
# They are `include_str!`d, which is exactly why they need this.
crates/tga-report/assets/report.css -text
crates/tga-report/assets/report.js  -text

# **cmd's `goto` cannot find a label in an LF-only batch file.** It fails with
# "The system cannot find the batch label specified" partway through a run that
# has already done its work -- which is how the analyser's `save.bat` first
# shipped.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Never touch the typefaces. A CRLF conversion inside a TTF is a font that no
# longer parses, and the failure surfaces as a window in the wrong typeface with
# no error anywhere.
*.ttf binary

# The icons and the HTML export's images, for the same reason: text detection
# that guesses wrong on a run of bytes is an image that draws as garbage.
*.ico binary
*.png binary
